  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Considering Preloaded Patterns**\n\nI\'m now leaning towards creating a dedicated library for preloaded patterns. The goal is to house sample mathematical coordinates, images, and functions. I\'m considering `/src/utils.ts` or `/src/data/patterns.ts` as potential locations for this library. Alternatively, I can embed drawing tools within the simulator or generate these patterns programmatically.\n\n\n**Expanding Visual Library**\n\nI\'m incredibly excited about the potential of the simulator now! I\'ve been brainstorming visual patterns to support. I\'m thinking of adding a biohazard symbol, a dynamic analog clock, a double helical vortex, custom text, and an Earth globe map. Additionally, I figured out how to support uploaded images! The user uploads an image, then pixel data gets mapped into the angular and radial sections corresponding to the 45 LEDs per physical strip. I think this will greatly enhance the media upload support and rotational coordinate mapping. I\'m now thinking about designing `/src/components/PovSimulator.tsx`.\n\n\n**Simulating Physics of Rotation**\n\nI\'m now diving deep into simulating the physics of POV displays! I\'m planning to use an accumulation canvas and calculate arm rotation based on RPM and delta-time. I\'ll be drawing glowing circular points with radial-decay to mimic retinal persistence. LED colors will come from sampling the target image at corresponding polar coordinates. I am also considering two arms that are separated.\n\n\n**Defining Arm Staggering**\n\nI\'ve been working on defining the physical arrangement of the LED strips, focusing on how to achieve high density and visual quality. I\'ve decided on \"3 parallel LED strips per arm: Left, Center, Right, staggered by 1.2mm\" to provide good image coverage. I\'m now formalizing the calculations for determining the exact position of each LED based on its arm, strip, and index. I\'ve defined formulas for calculating the angle and radius of each LED using stagger offsets, and have described the sampling process at $(x, y)$.\n\n\n**Implementing Motion Blur**\n\nI\'ve been working on retinal persistence, opting for semi-transparent black fills for realistic motion blur. I\'m excited by how the image dynamically degrades when the motor stops, or merges into a hologram with high-speed spinning. I\'m focusing on `/src/components/PovSimulator.tsx`, planning an offscreen 2D canvas for original pattern rendering.\n\n\n**Sub-Stepping Frame Rendering**\n\nI\'m implementing sub-stepping within each frame to simulate rotational physics accurately. Delta Theta is calculated as $\\omega \\cdot \\Delta t$, where $\\omega = 2\\pi \\cdot \\frac{\\text{RPM}}{60}$ rad/s. I\'m interpolating angle steps to handle high rotational speeds, even though the browser runs at 60 Hz. I am now subdividing the angle step into multiple finer increments per frame with fading opacity.\n\n\n**Refining Pattern Sampling**\n\nI\'m now nailing down how the simulator will sample patterns! I\'m creating a `getPatternPixel(x, y, patternType, timeMs, customText)` function, ensuring it gives back the RGB values. The coordinate system will be from $-1.0$ to $+1.0$. I\'ve got preloaded designs like a test card, a spinning globe, a matrix spiral, and a tech clock!\n\n\n"
    modified_response: "I will create the `/src/components/PovSimulator.tsx` file. This component implements an extremely realistic, physically accurate Persistence of Vision (POV) simulation. It models the integration of light over time (retinal persistence) by sub-stepping the angular displacement of the arms at the current RPM, sampling a Cartesian coordinate space mapped directly onto the physical layout of 2 arms, each containing 3 physical strips with 45 LEDs each (total of 270 simulated LEDs). When the RPM is zero or low, it separates into static lines; at commercial RPMs (900-1500), it merges into a volumetric holographic display. It also supports image uploads, text input, and animated patterns."
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 11
      nanos: 111836903
    }
