    thinking: "**Examining Existing Implementations**\n\nI\'m currently focused on `PovSimulator.tsx`, specifically lines 194-200. My objective is to refine the LED persistence and motion blur handling for better control over visual effects. Right now, I\'m diving into the existing motion blur and persistence implementations to understand how they currently function.\n\n\n**Refining Blur and Persistence**\n\nI\'m now considering the motion blur implementation within the code. While the current black overlay approach is basic, I see opportunities for improvement. Sub-pixel sampling and arc-rendering, or even rendering a sector wedge with alpha decay, would enhance the physics model\'s accuracy, better simulating retina phosphorus decay. I\'m also thinking of allowing user adjustment of both persistence and decay rates.\n\n\n**Implementing New Parameters**\n\nI\'m aiming to integrate new parameters into the simulation. Specifically, I plan to introduce \"Final Decay Rate,\" \"Motion Arc Blur Width,\" and \"Phosphor Glow Style,\" allowing finer control over the visual effects. I will be updating the `SimulationConfig` interface in `types.ts` to accommodate these additions. The plan is also to expose these parameters for user adjustment within `PovSimulator.tsx`, which will involve updating the UI controls. Finally, I will focus on implementing the exponential decay model in the drawing logic to achieve more realistic visual persistence.\n\n\n**Incorporating Physical Decay**\n\nI\'ve discovered that `ledPersistenceMs` in `SimulationConfig` is currently unused! I am planning to leverage this to model physical retinal decay accurately. The implementation will involve calculating a time-dependent decay rate using `dt` and `ledPersistenceMs`, ensuring consistent visual persistence across different frame rates. Specifically, I intend to apply an exponential decay factor calculated as `decayFactor = Math.pow(0.5, dt / (config.ledPersistenceMs / 1000))` to the canvas pixels. This will create a truly physics-based visual experience.\n\n\n**Calculating Decay Opacity**\n\nI\'ve been working on a precise and efficient method for frame-rate-independent visual decay in Canvas. I\'ve landed on using an exponential decay model, where I can read or write the pixel values. The decay opacity is determined as `1 - Math.exp(-dt * (1000 / config.ledPersistenceMs))`, offering accurate physics-based persistence modeling that is also computationally fast. For example, if persistence is 80ms and FPS is 60 (dt=0.0166), then the decay opacity is approximately 0.188, creating visual realism.\n\n\n**Confirming Rate Independence**\n\nI\'ve just confirmed the beauty of the exponential decay model! It perfectly models eye-phosphor decay, independent of frame rates, which is awesome. The decay rate is consistent in real-time seconds. I\'ve double-checked the formulas, and the result is great. Now, I will look at adding motion blur, and the impact of the existing velocity factor. Specifically, I\'m reviewing lines 499-502.\n\n\n**Implementing Arc Drawing**\n\nI\'m now focused on implementing arc drawing for LEDs, specifically a continuous arc or Bezier sweep for each LED within the sub-step to represent its motion. I\'m exploring the feasibility and efficiency of drawing arcs versus individual circles. The code will involve calculating arc paths from `angleStart` to `angleEnd` for each LED\'s position, using `ctx.arc()` to create a more realistic motion blur effect. I\'m excited about this approach!\n\n\n**Adding Arc Blurring**\n\nI\'m now determining the implementation for arc blurring. It appears drawing continuous arcs for each LED is more realistic and efficient than individual circles. I will calculate arc paths from the start and end angles of the LED\'s motion for each sub-step. I will use `ctx.arc()` to create a more authentic motion blur effect, and integrate a slider for \"LED Arc Blur Width\" to control the sweep length dynamically. I want to include the option of a dotted grid vs. continuous arc sweep.\n\n\n**Refining User Controls**\n\nI am now focusing on refining the user controls in `PovSimulator.tsx`. I\'ve found an existing \"Retinal Integration (Persistence)\" slider that adjusts `config.motionBlur`. My plan is to expand these controls to include a dedicated slider for `ledPersistenceMs`, with a range of `20ms` to `300ms`, which will allow for fine-tuning of retinal persistence. I also want to introduce a separate control, perhaps `motionBlurExponent`, to better refine the motion arc blur. I will need to refine these parameters in the code.\n\n\n**Adding Persistence Controls**\n\nI\'m now integrating user controls for `ledPersistenceMs` within `PovSimulator.tsx`. I\'ve added a slider from 20ms to 300ms, enabling precise adjustments for retinal persistence. I will also be looking into real-time telemetry graphing on the web dashboard to see key data. I\'m checking the current usage of `simConfig` and `ledPersistenceMs`, and also looking at adding an exponential decay mode selection.\n\n\n**Integrating Telemetry Graphs**\n\nI\'m now planning real-time telemetry graphs within the web dashboard, specifically `DashboardControl.tsx`. My idea is to replace or expand the \"Active Operational Telemetry\" panel with dynamic SVG path graphs to visualize data, starting with RPM, CPU load, and temperature. I will use a history array in React state to store and display the telemetry data in real-time.\n\n\n**Designing Physics Responsiveness**\n\nI\'m now focusing on designing the physics responsiveness for the telemetry graphs. I\'m working to make the simulation feel like real hardware, so I\'ll be updating telemetry data dynamically within `DashboardControl.tsx`. The RPM will change with rotor activity; temperature will rise and fall based on RPM; and CPU load will scale with LED count and brightness. I am also planning an `useEffect` hook to update the telemetry points in React state, every 200ms or faster for active