d subtle horizontal references. I\'m aiming for a visually appealing and informative real-time display.\n\n\n**Designing Error Handling**\n\nI\'m now incorporating error handling into the ESP32 firmware design. Specifically, I\'m focusing on robust Wi-Fi connection error states. The goal is to implement checks for `WiFi.status()` within a timeout loop during connection setup. Upon failure, the firmware will log a critical error message to the web dashboard, ensuring graceful degradation and diagnostic visibility. I\'m also planning to enhance the `FirmwareGenerator.tsx` to include explicit C++ functions for this error handling.\n\n\n\n**Implementing Error States**\n\nI\'m now concentrating on implementing error handling within the ESP32 firmware. Specifically, I plan to create a standalone `softAP` upon primary Wi-Fi connection failure, setting a `wifi_error_state` flag and broadcasting an error message. I\'ll also implement a background thread to continually attempt reconnection and handle disconnected events. For Hall sensor errors, I\'ll monitor interrupt intervals, disable FastLED outputs upon timeout, and log sensor failure messages.\n\n\n**Adding Error Panel**\n\nI\'m now integrating error handling and fault injection controls. I plan to add a \"**FAULT RECOVERY & INJECTION CONTROL**\" panel in `DashboardControl.tsx`, enabling Wi-Fi dropout and Hall sensor failure simulation. The goal is to make a high-fidelity simulator that reflects physical behavior. This will enhance diagnostic visibility.\n\n\n**Simulating Error Injection**\n\nI\'m now focused on implementing the fault injection controls in `DashboardControl.tsx`. I am planning to simulate various failure scenarios for enhanced diagnostic capabilities. This includes simulating Wi-Fi dropouts, Hall sensor stalls, and voltage brownouts, providing realistic responses to user actions. I\'ll need to define log sequences and set relevant state flags.\n\n\n**Reviewing Thermal Mitigation**\n\nI\'m now implementing the thermal mitigation display. I\'ll add a warning banner to `PovSimulator.tsx`, displaying a message when LED brightness is throttled. I also want to check the current state of files. I will make sure not to add too much file-path noise or unrequested features. This is going to be a highly crafted, non-slop technical solution!\n\n\n**Implementing Thermal Mitigation**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the thermal mitigation implementation. I\'m adding a warning banner to `PovSimulator.tsx` to notify when LED brightness is throttled, and will ensure file changes remain clean and focused on essential features. I am going for a highly refined technical solution!\n\n\n**Calculating Visual Decay**\n\nI\'ve successfully implemented a frame-rate-independent visual decay model using exponential decay, resulting in a physically accurate and computationally efficient solution. The new code leverages `ledPersistenceMs` to provide realistic eye-phosphor simulation. This ensures consistent visual retention regardless of browser frame rate.\n\n\n**Analyzing LED Rendering**\n\nI\'m now analyzing the current LED rendering implementation, specifically lines 497-522 of `PovSimulator.tsx`, to understand its approach to velocity and intensity. The goal is to refine the `physicalVelocityFactor` and `intensityGlow` calculations to better simulate physical LED behavior and visual effects. I\'m considering enhancements that accurately model motion blur and phosphor glow.\n\n\n**Implementing Arc Strokes**\n\nI\'m now implementing the arc drawing for the LEDs\' motion blur, specifically within the frame interval. I\'m focusing on calculating `deltaAngle` from `isPlaying` and `rpm` to sweep from the previous to the current LED position. By calculating the arc path, I can draw the exact physical arc sweep using `ctx.beginPath()` and other appropriate methods. This will enhance the visual realism!\n\n\n**Refining Arc Rendering**\n\nI\'m now integrating continuous arc drawing for motion blur, calculating `deltaAngle` to sweep from previous to current LED positions. This involves using `ctx.beginPath()` and other methods for physically accurate arc rendering. I\'m aiming for enhanced visual realism and integration of the spiral, clock, and text at high speeds. This will allow integration of visual effects.\n\n\n**Adding New Controls**\n\nI\'m now integrating new UI controls into `PovSimulator.tsx`. I\'m adding dedicated sliders for `ledPersistenceMs`, controlling retinal decay, and another for `Physical Arc Blur Intensity`. This will allow me to refine the simulation, directly linking `config.ledPersistenceMs` to the \"Physic Retinal Decay Rate\" slider and \"Physical Arc Blur Intensity\" to another new slider for visual adjustments. I am currently considering the integration between `motionBlur` and other parameters.\n\n\n"
