    thinking: "**Refining The Strategy**\n\nI\'ve decided to proceed by replacing most of `DashboardControl.tsx` with a single `edit_file` call. The sheer simplicity of this approach is appealing; it allows a fresh start and avoids complex, potentially error-prone incremental changes within the existing, lengthy file. The size of the file, about 285 lines, makes a full replace feel like a clean sweep. I\'m excited by this robust, straightforward strategy.\n\n\n**Defining State Variables**\n\nNow, I\'m meticulously defining the state variables for the new implementation. I\'ve enumerated them, including booleans, numbers with default values, strings, and the crucial `history` array for the graph, initialized with 30 pre-constructed elements. I am also checking how to make the history elements pristine. I\'m taking care to ensure they are all appropriately typed for ease of use.\n\n\n**Constructing Chart Elements**\n\nI\'m now focused on constructing the elements for the SVG graph. I\'m carefully outlining the `drawSvgPath` function, considering how to handle empty history and how to map data to a visual representation within a defined width and height. I\'m establishing scaling parameters, like `minVal` and `maxVal`, for the three tabs, `rpm`, `cpu`, and `temp`, and the corresponding `getVal` function to extract the relevant data from the history array.\n\n\n**Calculating Path Coordinates**\n\nI\'m deep in the mathematics of the SVG path. I am finalizing the `drawSvgFillPath` function, ensuring it correctly calculates and returns path coordinates based on the history data and scaling parameters. I\'m focusing on handling edge cases, and making sure that empty history is gracefully handled. I\'m making sure to define different getVal functions, for each tab, to ensure that the correct data is processed.\n\n\n**Confirming Responsive Scaling**\n\nI\'ve just confirmed the responsive scaling using `viewBox`, ensuring the SVG renders perfectly within any container. This is a robust solution. I\'m excited that this handles responsiveness elegantly. Now I\'m refining the SVG helper details, adding vertical reference grids at 10%, 50%, and 90% with corresponding labels, and a blinking pulsar circle at the end of the trace to indicate ongoing streaming.\n\n\n"
    modified_response: "I will rewrite `DashboardControl.tsx` to incorporate a dual-mode Captive Portal telemetry display (toggling between raw figures and glowing real-time SVG vector wave plots), implement a continuous physics-based ramping engine, and add custom error hardware fault injectors to log Wi-Fi failures, Hall sensor stalls, and power brownouts."
