    thinking: "**Refining Error Handling**\n\nI\'m currently focused on improving the error handling within the C++ firmware, specifically regarding Wi-Fi connection issues. The existing retry mechanism (`wifiTimeout < 15`) is a decent starting point. However, I am now thinking about how to integrate a non-blocking background reconnection process using Wi-Fi event handler groups. The goal is to ensure the primary rendering loop is never interrupted by Wi-Fi connection attempts.\n\n\n**Implementing Error Mitigation**\n\nI\'ve been working on hardening the firmware\'s error handling. I\'m adding a `wifi_error_state` variable to `main.cpp` for more detailed logging and potential softAP launch. I\'m also adding a new suite of stall checks to `highSpeedRenderingLoop` to prevent pixel burn-in, with logging, alerts, and buffer flushes. And I\'m implementing a C++ state machine for interrupt checking. Finally, I\'ve got FastLED power throttling protection working and integrated!\n\n\n**Integrating Power Limits**\n\nI\'ve integrated the FastLED power limiting directly into the `begin()` method of `POV_Engine` within `rendering_engine.h`. To improve reliability, I\'m adding `POV_Engine::checkLineContinuity()`, a helper function to validate DMA pin assignments and the DMA initialization, with error handling and fallback mechanisms for safety. My next steps are to integrate these changes into the code generation process in `/src/components/FirmwareGenerator.tsx`, starting with an analysis of `generateMainCode()` between lines 75 and 253, so I can clean it up.\n\n\n**Designing System Monitoring**\n\nI\'m now focusing on system monitoring! I\'ve designed a production-ready Wi-Fi reconnection system using ESP32 event handlers to automatically reconnect on disconnect. Next, I\'m working on the Hall Sensor interrupt stall detection, and LED spot protection features, to prevent potential damage. I am also implementing error messaging to the developer.\n\n\n**Implementing Stall Prevention**\n\nI\'m now implementing stall prevention by adding an interrupt trigger period check, and implementing protective measures like brightness reduction and clearing the LEDs if a stall is detected. I\'m also implementing a delay. I\'m prioritizing safety and CPU clock cycle conservation during these stalls. I\'m aiming to minimize potential thermal damage to the stator layers. I\'m using an if statement to avoid an error condition when the device is initialized.\n\n\n**Analyzing Power Budget**\n\nI am now analyzing the power budget constraints. I\'ve realized WS2812B LEDs consume significant current, and the slip rings have a strict limit. I\'m focusing on leveraging FastLED\'s power calculations, which are used to dynamically throttle brightness and preserve VCC voltage levels. I\'m working on how to integrate this with the hardware FastLED initialisation, checking pin allocations to prevent GPIO conflicts. If I encounter issues, I\'ll fall back to safe defaults. I\'m integrating checks for pin ranges to ensure hardware safety.\n\n\n"
