  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Identifying the SPA**\n\nI\'ve confirmed this is a single-page application, which changes my approach. The ESP32 code residing in `FirmwareGenerator.tsx` is crucial, as it\'s directly presented to the user as generated C++ firmware source. The user can customize, download, or copy the generated C++ code directly.\n\n\n**Analyzing Client-Side Simulation**\n\nI\'ve realized the \"Web Dashboard\" is client-side simulated, a key design insight. This changes the focus to ensuring correct C++ firmware generation within `FirmwareGenerator.tsx`. I now need to add checks for FastLED, Hall sensor, and Wi-Fi errors within the generated C++ code. The \"live simulated web dashboard\" in `DashboardControl.tsx` and `PovSimulator.tsx` must be updated to visualize these simulated errors.\n\n\n**Refining Error Visualization**\n\nI\'m now focused on enhancing the error visualization within the client-side simulation. Specifically, I\'m working to make the fault generators in `DashboardControl.tsx` more interactive. They should log events, trigger graphs, adjust POV simulator physics (LED persistence, speed, decay), and display error logs in the Terminal Console block to mimic firmware behavior. I\'m reviewing `DashboardControl.tsx` and recently added checkpoint notes to ensure this is implemented as requested.\n\n\n**Implementing Error Checks**\n\nI\'m now implementing the requested error checks within the ESP32 firmware generation in `FirmwareGenerator.tsx`. I\'ll focus on adding FastLED communication error checks, Hall sensor failure detection, and Wi-Fi connection failure logging. I\'m focusing on the `generateEngineCode()` function within `FirmwareGenerator.tsx`, specifically looking at the lines representing C++ files like `main.cpp`.\n\n\n**Examining ESP32 Hall Checks**\n\nI\'m now reviewing the existing hall sensor interrupt handler and thinking about fail-safe checks. The goal is to detect stalled states\342\200\224no interrupts for 500ms. I\'m considering a watchdog timer or task loop check within `highSpeedRenderingLoop` to trigger a safe state. I\'m focusing on modifying the `generateMainCode` function in `FirmwareGenerator.tsx` from lines 75 to 286, to integrate this safety measure.\n\n\n**Adding WebSocket Fault Reporting**\n\nI\'m now integrating WebSocket communication to report Hall sensor faults, as I\'ve already implemented the stall detection logic. I\'ll include sending a JSON event to connected clients. I\'ve added a WebSocket message to notify all clients, ensuring they receive the \"sensor_fault\" event when the sensor stalls. This will allow for real-time error visualization, which is an important step.\n\n\n**Implementing Recovery Detection**\n\nI\'m now integrating logic for Hall sensor fault recovery within the ESP32 firmware. Specifically, I am focusing on detecting when a stalled state has ended, and the sensor is again providing interrupts within `highSpeedRenderingLoop`. I\'m going to add a WebSocket event to notify clients with a \"sensor_recovered\" event, containing a message. I\'m focusing on lines 32 to 43 in `FirmwareGenerator.tsx`, the `if (newRevolutionFlag)` block to handle recovery messages.\n\n\n**Designing a Watchdog Timer**\n\nI\'m now designing a watchdog timer using an ESP32 hardware timer interrupt, as I realized the interrupt handler can\'t detect a lack of interrupts. The timer will trigger every 500ms. In the hall interrupt service routine, I\'ll reset the timer to implement the watchdog pattern. I\'m ready to write this C++ code.\n\n\n\n**Implementing Watchdog Timer**\n\nI\'m now implementing the watchdog timer using the ESP32 hardware timers. I\'ve defined `sensorWatchdogTimer`, `sensorFaultActive`, and `totalInterruptMissCount`. The `onWatchdogTimerMatch` function sets `sensorFaultActive` when the timer expires. In setup, I initialize the timer for a 500ms timeout using `timerBegin`, `timerAttachInterrupt`, and `timerAlarmWrite`. The `handleHallSensorInterrupt` function will reset the timer, I think.\n\n\n**Adding FastLED Error Checks**\n\nI\'m now integrating FastLED communication error checks into the ESP32 firmware. My focus is on implementing checksum validations or DMA state checks within the FastLED library to detect potential data line glitches. I\'ll log these detected errors via WebSocket to the dashboard, indicating which LED strip might be malfunctioning, per the requirement. I will explore FastLED\'s internal mechanisms to see how to implement this.\n\n\n**Integrating Sector Faults**\n\nI\'m now integrating sector fault detection into the rendering engine, focusing on the `renderSector` function within `rendering_engine.h`. I\'ll analyze the C++ code to potentially add checksum validation or error checking for flash memory reads within the sector interruptions, aiming to identify data corruption during sector rendering. I\'ll need to define how to handle any identified errors, e.g., via WebSocket, so I can display on the simulated POV display.\n\n\n**Examining FastLED Error Checks**\n\nI\'m now investigating FastLED\'s internal mechanisms for error handling, as it doesn\'t throw exceptions. I will add a `checkStripCommunication()` function to detect communication glitches by examining the FastLED controller status flags or pin responses. I\'m focusing on checksum validation or DMA status checks within `show_esp32_dma()` to detect transmission failures. I\'ll need to explore the specific flags and how to use them.\n\n\n**Implementing LED Bus Checks**\n\nI\'m now implementing `verifyBusIntegrity()` within the `POV_Engine` to check for LED strip communication errors, as I\'ve found it\'s not possible to leverage checksums. I am writing code to check for signal mismatches, high capacitance reflections, or pin shorts. The goal is to detect and report FastLED glitches via WebSocket messages. I\'m focusing on the code related to pin states within the ESP32 registers.\n\n\n**Detecting LED Glitches**\n\nI\'m now implementing the `verifyBusIntegrity` function within `POV_Engine` to check for LED strip communication errors and writing code to check for signal mismatches, high capacitance reflections, or pin shorts. I\'m leveraging `gpio_get_level` to detect an unexpected signal clamp, indicative of a data line short. These FastLED glitches will be reported to the dashboard via WebSocket messages. I\'m focusing on lines I\'ve added to `POV_Engine` to report issues on the WebSocket bus.\n\n\n**Adding Wi-Fi Error Logging**\n\nI\'m now integrating robust Wi-Fi error logging into the ESP32 firmware. I\'m leveraging LittleFS to create a persistent log file, `/wifi_err.log`, to record connection failures such as incorrect SSIDs or passwords. I\'ve added a helper function to handle LittleFS initialization, open the log file, and write detailed connection failure messages. I will include functionality to send these logs via WebSocket when the connection is re-established.\n\n\n**Finalizing Wi-Fi Log Sync**\n\nI\'ve completed integrating Wi-Fi error logging and synchronization. I\'ve added error handling for LittleFS initialization and file operations. I\'m now leveraging LittleFS to create a persistent log file, `/wifi_err.log`, to record connection failures such as incorrect SSIDs or passwords. I\'ve included functionality to send these logs via WebSocket when the connection is re-established.\n\n\n**Logging Wi-Fi Connection Failures**\n\nI\'m now integrating detailed logging for Wi-Fi connection failures. Specifically, I\'m modifying `onWiFiEvent` to catch `ARDUINO_EVENT_WIFI_STA_DISCONNECTED` and extract failure reasons. I\'m focusing on handling cases like `WIFI_REASON_NO_AP_FOUND`, `WIFI_REASON_AUTH_EXPIRE`, and `WIFI_REASON_BEACON_TIMEOUT`, providing specific error messages. I will then log these reasons using LittleFS.\n\n\n**Verifying LittleFS Integration**\n\nI\'m now verifying the LittleFS integration for Wi-Fi logging. I\'ve added `#include <LittleFS.h>` to `main.cpp` and added an initialization check to `setup()` to ensure it mounts properly with `LittleFS.begin(true)`. I\'ve also updated `onWiFiEvent()` to transmit logs when a connection is re-established. I will now double-check the integration and build.\n\n\n**Refining WiFi Event Handling**\n\nI\'m now refining the Wi-Fi event handler and ensuring proper signature usage. I\'m focusing on the `onWiFiEvent` function to ensure its signature matches the modern ESP32 Arduino core. I\'ll verify the event handling logic to log specific failure reasons using LittleFS for persistent storage and WebSocket for real-time notification. I\'m reviewing the `FirmwareGenerator.tsx` file for relevant areas.\n\n\n**Refactoring Configuration Usage**\n\nI\'m now refactoring the `generateMainCode()` function within `FirmwareGenerator.tsx`, focusing on consolidating and correctly incorporating configuration variables. I\'m working to ensure that the variables `config.ssid`, `config.wifiPass`, `config.hostname`, `config.pinLedArm1`, `config.pinLedArm2`, `config.pinHallSensor`, and `config.ledsPerStrip` are correctly inserted at lines 75-287. I will then review the resulting C++ code.\n\nI am also making sure that if a default is provided for the configs, that those defaults are appropriately used in the generated code.\n\n\n**Incorporating Firmware Configuration**\n\nI\'m now incorporating firmware configuration into the code generation process within `FirmwareGenerator.tsx`. I am ensuring that hardware and networking configs, such as SSID, password, hostname, and pin definitions are correctly passed into the C++ code I generate. I\'ve focused on using the default configs, if a specific configuration is not provided to the ESP32.\n\n\n**Enhancing Fault Resilience**\n\nI\'m now integrating a background network event handler to ensure robust Wi-Fi restoration. I\'m focusing on the `onWiFiEvent` function to manage Wi-Fi connection events, specifically logging disconnects and reconnecting. I\'ll utilize `WIFI_SSID` and `WIFI_PASS` to initiate reconnection attempts. This is important to ensure that the POV is operational. I\'m also ensuring the high-speed rendering loop has guards for hall-probe failures.\n\n\n**Finalizing Event Handling**\n\nI\'m now finalizing the implementation of the sensor fault handling. I have added fault detection within the high-speed rendering loop to respond to stalled states. I have added code to set brightness, clear the LEDs, and delay the thread. I am focused on the lines in `main.cpp` I\'ve just added. The generated C++ is now more robust.\n\n\n**Finalizing Code Integration**\n\nI\'m now integrating the final pieces, focusing on the `highSpeedRenderingLoop` in `main.cpp` and WebSocket message handling. I\'m verifying the LED strip, Wi-Fi, and Hall sensor. I\'ve integrated fault handling to set brightness, clear LEDs, and delay threads to improve resilience.\n\n\n**Verifying LED Communication**\n\nI\'m now verifying LED strip communication, confirming signal integrity. I\'ve focused on testing the `verifyBusIntegrity` functionality I added, looking for unexpected signal clamps or high capacitance issues. I\'ll test the LED bus using the function I just wrote.\n\n\n**Reviewing Telemetry Data**\n\nI\'m now reviewing the existing telemetry data being sent via the API and WebSocket. I\'m focusing on the data points transmitted like RPM, brightness, temperature, voltage, Wi-Fi status, and sensor faults. I need to make sure the data is accurate and correctly formatted. I am considering what I might add.\n\n\n**Refining Config Handling**\n\nI\'m now refining how the firmware config variables are handled during C++ code generation. My focus is on ensuring the `generateMainCode()` function within `FirmwareGenerator.tsx` correctly handles hardware and networking configs, especially default values. I\'m carefully reviewing the integration of `config.ssid`, `config.wifiPass`, and related pins to the generated C++ to maintain robustness.\n\n\n"
