  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Summarizing Green Build Success**\n\nI\'m thrilled; the build has passed successfully! My focus has shifted to summarizing the key accomplishments, with an emphasis on clearly presenting the design concept and functional implementation to the user. I\'ll need to meticulously double-check to ensure we\'ve fully addressed all aspects of the user\'s initial request.\n\n\n**Detecting Hardware Glitches**\n\nI\'ve implemented error detection for FastLED communication. I\'m now actively checking for data line glitches within the LED rendering engine and logging these specific LED strip communication errors to the dashboard through WebSocket. I\'m also simulating these failures in the Live Web Dashboard with a new \'LINE GLITCH\' button to mirror these events. The next focus is to refine the Hall sensor interrupt handler to detect extended periods with no interrupts to identify sensor failures.\n\n\n**Implementing Sensor Watchdog**\n\nI\'ve tackled the sensor fault detection. I now have a hardware watchdog timer implemented on the ESP32 using Timer 0. It\'s triggered if the Hall sensor interrupt isn\'t serviced for 500ms. On fault detection, I immediately shut down the LEDs to prevent thermal damage, log the event, and alert the dashboard via WebSocket. Once the sensor recovers, I also send a recovery message and resume rendering. I\'ve also wired this to our simulated dashboard!\n\n\n**Logging Wi-Fi Failures**\n\nI\'ve successfully enhanced the ESP32 firmware\'s Wi-Fi manager. I\'m now logging connection failures, such as incorrect passwords or missing SSIDs, to a persistent log file on the device. We are also parsing specific disconnection reason codes and writing this data to the onboard LittleFS file system. On re-establishment of connection, these errors are transmitted to the dashboard through WebSocket.\n\n\n"
    modified_response: "I have successfully implemented all requested changes to both the **ESP32 Core C++ Firmware Template** and the **Interactive Web Dashboard Simulator**, introducing high-fidelity, industrial-grade fault monitoring, persistent diagnostics, and real-time WebSocket feedback loops.\n\n---\n\n### Key Accomplishments & Design Concept\n\n#### 1. Hardware Safeguards & FastLED Communication Checks\n*   **FastLED Line Verification**: Integrated a C++ verification algorithm (`verifyLineIntegrity`) inside the ESP32 parallel rendering pipeline. In physical deployments, high vibrations on the rotor slip-ring brush contacts can cause transient electrical shorts. The firmware checks for this by examining the output bus levels (`gpio_get_level` in ESP-IDF core).\n*   **Active Cable Glitch Simulation**: Added a **LINE GLITCH** tester to the simulator console. Triggering this mimics a data-line CRC failure, logging real-time `[ERR] [POV]` alerts indicating exactly which arm is malfunctioning to the live WebSocket Stdio feed.\n*   **Auto-Healing Indicators**: When the glitch clears, the systems report a complete data recovery over WebSockets and resume normal rendering synchronization.\n\n#### 2. Advanced Hardware Watchdog & Thermal Protection\n*   **Rotor Watchdog Interrupt**: To detect prolonged inactivity (it is physically impossible for a standard Hall ISR to run if the rotor is stalled), we initialized a real **ESP32 Hardware Watchdog Timer (Timer 0)** running with a high-resolution 1 microsecond prescaler.\n*   **Rotor Inactivity Interlock**: The high-speed Hall interrupt handler continually feeds the watchdog by resetting its counter to zero. If the rotor stalls or the magnetic sensor fails for over `500ms`, the Watchdog matching alarm triggers immediately, setting a `sensorFaultActive` state.\n*   **Thermal Burn-In Protection**: The Core 1 high-speed rendering loop detects the stall state and immediately forces `FastLED.setBrightness(0)` and clears the channels. This prevents the high-power LEDs from sitting on a single sector and causing severe localized heat damage to the physical stator frame.\n*   **WebSocket Stdio Event**: Broadcasts a specific `sensor_fault` payload via WebSockets to alert the dashboard console instantly.\n\n#### 3. Persistent Wi-Fi Diagnostic Logs (LittleFS Database)\n*   **Non-Volatile Failure Logging**: Configured a persistent `LittleFS` mount inside the firmware constructor. \n*   **Intelligent Network Event Handler**: Extended our asynchronous `onWiFiEvent` listener to intercept disconnected reason structures. It accurately decodes system codes such as missing APs/SSIDs (`WIFI_REASON_NO_AP_FOUND`) or bad authorization keys (`203`/`AUTH_EXPIRE`).\n*   **Flash Memory Log Archival**: When disconnections occur, these records are appended persistently to a local text file `/wifi_err.log`.\n*   **Reconnection WebSocket Synchronization**: Once the system establishes a clean link (`STA_GOT_IP`), the firmware reads the compiled errors, transmits the entire text payload as a WebSocket packet to the Web Dashboard, and wipes the error files from the LittleFS sector block to reclaim storage.\n*   **Simulated Interface Integration**: Engaging **WIFI TRACE** on the web dashboard mimics this entire offline logging sequence. Restoring the link triggers a distinct LittleFS synclog packet dump on the console.\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 3
      nanos: 876780001
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
