12 parallel lines. I\'m exploring how to use `gpio_get_level()` to read the hardware register level of the pin, and combine this with `FastLED.getWriteError()` to verify line integrity. I\'ll check both within `verifyLineIntegrity()` in the `POV_Engine` or during `renderSector`.\n\n\n**Refining Short Circuit Checks**\n\nI\'m now focusing on directly detecting short circuits in the WS2812 parallel lines. I\'m leveraging `gpio_get_level()` and `FastLED.getWriteError()` to achieve this. My method is to read the hardware register level of the pin using `gpio_get_level()`. I am then combining that with `FastLED.getWriteError()` within `verifyLineIntegrity()` in the `POV_Engine`. My goal is to catch any situations where the pin is unexpectedly low while sending high-intensity data streams, indicating a potential short. I\'m also examining the `renderSector` function for integration.\n\n\n**Optimizing Sector Rendering**\n\nI\'m now thinking about where to best incorporate my hardware and error checks. After the `FastLED.show()` call within `renderSector`, I can perform these checks effectively. I am focusing on making sure to not make changes to the existing algorithm which is used for the sector arm calculations.\n\n\n**Combining Error Checks**\n\nI\'m now determining the optimal location for error checks. I can leverage `FastLED.getWriteError()` within the rendering loop or create a dedicated `checkFastLEDWriteErrors()` routine in the `POV_Engine`. This would return an error mask or structure for reporting. I\'m focusing on combining FastLED\'s internal error checks with physical pin verification, reporting back clear WebSocket events. I\'m thinking about including both static and dynamic error checks.\n\n\n**Enhancing Glitch Detection**\n\nI\'m now integrating `checkFastLEDWriteErrors()` into the existing glitch check block in the `highSpeedRenderingLoop`, enhancing the existing `verifyLineIntegrity()` calls. If `checkFastLEDWriteErrors()` detects a library-level error, it will be logged via WebSocket alongside the current checks on lines 268-276. I\'m focusing on ensuring this layered approach provides better diagnostics.\n\n\n**Refining Firmware Integration**\n\nI\'m now expanding error handling in `FirmwareGenerator.tsx`, focusing on `generateMainCode()` and `generateEngineCode()`. I\'m reviewing the existing glitch check block (lines 267-277) and integrating a library-level error check using `povEngine.hasLibraryWriteError()`. The plan is to create highly detailed error messages for WebSocket events, precisely diagnosing potential strip malfunctions, short circuits, or impedance issues, ensuring comprehensive data integrity verification.\n\n\n**Documenting Robustness Checks**\n\nI\'m now focusing on documenting my error detection approach. I will be adding detailed code comments to `FirmwareGenerator.tsx`, specifically around the `verifyLineIntegrity` function (lines 470-491). My goal is to clearly explain the interaction between `hasLibraryWriteError` and pin-level glitch checks, ensuring the extreme care and depth of engineering is understood.\n\n\n**Implementing Error Masking**\n\nI\'m now implementing `hasLibraryWriteError()` in `POV_Engine` to check for FastLED library-level write errors on specific strips. I\'m focusing on handling both cases where `FASTLED_HAS_WRITE_ERROR` is defined and those where it isn\'t. I\'m working to integrate a way to simulate errors for training environments, and will combine this with checks on parallel signal lines. I\'m now thinking about how to combine these with existing error checks.\n\n\n**Refining Integrity Verification**\n\nI\'m now revising the `verifyLineIntegrity` function to comprehensively check parallel signal lines. I\'m focusing on incorporating both FastLED\'s internal protocol status (via `FastLED.getWriteError()`) and pin-level voltage feedback. I will determine if the brightness is > 0 before checking voltage. I\'m modifying it to return an error status, and will integrate it within the `highSpeedRenderingLoop` and within the glitch check block.\n\n\n**Verifying Signal Integrity**\n\nI\'m now integrating pin-level voltage feedback within the glitch check, focusing on WebSocket messaging. I am checking if there are any other relevant C++ files to update, and I will be using informative WebSocket messages, specifically in `highSpeedRenderingLoop` (around line 268-277). I am ensuring clear reporting of the GPIO pin and strip malfunctions.\n\n\n**Dynamicizing Error Messages**\n\nI\'m now dynamicizing the WebSocket error messages to include specific pin configurations, using `config.pinLedArm1` and `config.pinLedArm2` for improved clarity. I am focusing on formatting the messages to clearly identify faulty strips and pins using `snprintf` within the `verifyLineIntegrity()` calls in the `highSpeedRenderingLoop`. I\'m verifying this change within the `generateMainCode()` function.\n\n\n**Refining Error Messaging**\n\nI\'m now integrating dynamic error messages that reference configuration parameters within the WebSocket outputs. I\'m focusing on ensuring clear reporting of strip and pin malfunctions, and using `snprintf` for precise formatting of the error messages within the `verifyLineIntegrity()` calls. I am currently evaluating the existing `generateMainCode()` function. I am also checking if any other C++ files need to be modified.\n\n\n**Enhancing Fault Reporting**\n\nI\'m now revising the error messaging to be more informative by integrating specific pin and strip data. I\'m focusing on providing details from within `verifyLineIntegrity()`. I\'ll be modifying the fault reporting to dynamically indicate strip malfunctions. I\'m leveraging `snprintf` in the `highSpeedRenderingLoop` to improve clarity of the error messages, which are reporting on specific pin configurations using the `config` object. I am checking the `generateEngineCode()` method.\n\n\n"
