  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Considering Connection Protocols**\n\nI\'m currently focused on enabling users to connect to their ESP32 devices via WebSocket. I\'m thinking of adding an `esp32ConnectionMode` state variable within `DashboardControl.tsx`. This variable would toggle between `\'simulation\'` and `\'hardware\'` modes, and I will also need an `esp32Ip` variable to store the IP address.\n\n\n**Establishing WebSocket Connection**\n\nI\'ve decided to implement a WebSocket client within `DashboardControl.tsx` using the `useEffect` hook. I will also be including states for connection mode and IP address, and connection status. The connection will only be attempted when the connection mode is set to `\'hardware\'`. The WebSocket will then try to connect to the ESP32 via `ws://${espIp}:3000/ws`. This is where I am right now.\n\n\n**Implementing WebSocket Functionality**\n\nI\'ve been working on the `connect` function, crucial for handling the WebSocket connection. The plan is to create a new `WebSocket` instance and manage its lifecycle events. I\'ll need to set up handlers for `onopen`, `onmessage`, and other relevant events. When the connection opens successfully, I\'ll update the state, log a message, and synchronize the initial state of the system, sending data like RPM, brightness, pattern, and text over the socket.\n\n\n**Handling Socket Events**\n\nI\'m now focused on managing the different events I might receive via the WebSocket. I\'m adding specific handlers in `DashboardControl.tsx` for messages. I\'m thinking of how to handle sensor faults, LED faults, and network log synchronization, with different display states and log messages. I\'m especially interested in parsing log data sent by the ESP32 and displaying this in an accessible format.\n\n\n**Refining Error Handling**\n\nI\'m currently focused on improving error management within the WebSocket event handlers in `DashboardControl.tsx`. Specifically, I\'m working on the `onmessage` handler. I\'ve added a `try...catch` block to handle potential parsing errors in the incoming data. If the data isn\'t a valid JSON packet, I\'ll display the raw message as a log, which will be useful for debugging. Additionally, I\'ve implemented error logging for the `onerror` event, and a reconnection attempt on `onclose`, with a 4-second delay.\n\n\n**Implementing Command Dispatch**\n\nI\'m now designing `sendHardwareCommand`, the function that dispatches user actions to the hardware. I\'ve decided a `useRef` hook is the perfect way to store the active `WebSocket` instance, enabling easy broadcast of commands. I\'m updating `wsRef.current` in the `onopen` and `onclose` events to accurately reflect the WebSocket\'s connection state. This ensures that commands are sent to the correct socket or handled gracefully if the connection is down.\n\n\n"
    modified_response: "I will view the physical WebSocket parsing handler inside `/src/components/FirmwareGenerator.tsx` to ensure that any commands sent from our web & APK clients match the physical JSON schema expected by the real-time C++ rendering daemon. Let\'s call `view_file`."
