ngle operation.\n\n\n**Refining the UI**\n\nI\'m now refining the UI for device control, focusing on the connection mode buttons. I\'ve integrated buttons for both simulated and hardware ESP32 connections, with clear visual cues to indicate the active mode. I\'ve ensured a user-friendly experience by implementing a seamless transition between these modes. This will give the user complete and immediate control.\n\n\n**Adjusting Button Logic**\n\nI\'m now adjusting the button logic within the UI to properly toggle the hardware connection mode. I\'ve focused on ensuring the button labels and visual cues accurately reflect the active mode. I\'m also implementing the necessary state updates to manage the ESP32 IP address input field\'s visibility based on the selected connection mode. This is crucial for a smooth user experience.\n\n\n**Analyzing the Websocket State**\n\nI\'m now diving into the websocket client state display within the UI. I\'m focusing on the visual cues: analyzing how the colored circles and text dynamically update. This includes their states, their corresponding colors, and how they indicate connection status. The CSS classes play a pivotal role in illustrating the connection state.\n\n\n**Preparing the Captive Portal**\n\nI\'m now implementing the captive portal integration within the device control section. I\'m focusing on providing clear instructions for connecting to the ESP32\'s Wi-Fi, including the default SSID and password. The integration targets a specific chunk within the UI to ensure accurate placement. I\'m finalizing the structure to ensure seamless captive portal instructions.\n\n```\n```\n\n**Adding the Compilation Card**\n\nI\'m now integrating the APK compilation card into the React workspace. It is pre-packaged with Ionic Capacitor for native Android compilation. My focus is on providing a clear interface for real-time device control and telemetry via Wi-Fi. I want the compilation card to be user-friendly and clearly illustrate the